import MotionSystemLayout from '../../../layouts/MotionSystemLayout.astro';
---
<MotionSystemLayout
  title="Mammoth-3D AWD Gantry 300"
  creator="mammoth-3D-DevTeam"
  creatorUrl="https://github.com/mammoth-3D"
  projectUrl="https://github.com/mammoth-3D/Mammoth-FDM-MODs/tree/main/Voron%203D%20Printers/mammoth-3D%20AWD%20Gantry%20300%20V1.7.3"
  projectLabel="GitHub — mammoth-3D/Mammoth-FDM-MODs"
  license="GPL-3.0"
  targetPrinters={[
    { name: "Voron 2.4 300mm", notes: "Primary target; designed and tested for this build volume" },
  ]}
  alsoFitsOn={[
    { name: "Voron Trident 300mm", notes: "Minor modifications to Z attachment points required" },
  ]}
  motionType="AWD CoreXY"
  motors="4× NEMA17 — 2 additional vs stock for all-wheel-drive"
  beltSpec="6mm GT2 open loop"
  railSpec="MGN12H on X, MGN9C or MGN12H on Y"
  difficulty="Advanced"
  printedParts={40}
  printTime="~25–35 hours total"
  compatibleToolheads={[
    { name: "Voron Stealthburner", notes: "Primary" },
    { name: "CW2 Clockwork 2" },
    { name: "Dragon Burner", notes: "With adapter" },
    { name: "MGN12 toolhead carriages" },
  ]}
  communityResources={[
    {
      name: "mammoth-3D/Mammoth-FDM-MODs",
      url: "https://github.com/mammoth-3D/Mammoth-FDM-MODs",
      type: "GitHub",
    },
  ]}
  tags={["AWD", "CoreXY", "Voron", "gantry", "performance", "high-speed"]}
  excerpt="Mammoth-3D AWD Gantry 300 — all-wheel-drive CoreXY gantry mod for the Voron 2.4 300mm. Four motors, 40 printed parts, GPL-3.0."
>
  <p>
    AWD (all-wheel-drive) in the context of a CoreXY printer means driving all four belt attachment
    points with independent motors — two A-motors and two B-motors — rather than the standard
    configuration of one per axis. The Mammoth-3D AWD Gantry 300 implements this on a Voron 2.4
    300mm by replacing the stock single-motor-per-axis arrangement with paired motors at all four
    gantry corners. All four motors work together: on a pure X move, both B-motors spin the same
    direction while both A-motors oppose them; on a pure Y move, the arrangement reverses. The
    firmware (typically Klipper with a custom kinematics configuration) sums the forces at each
    belt anchor point in real time.
  </p>
  <p>
    The primary benefit of AWD over standard CoreXY is belt tension consistency under load. In a
    standard two-motor CoreXY, the single A-motor and single B-motor each drive a long, continuous
    belt path from one corner across the full gantry width. At high accelerations, the belt
    stretches asymmetrically — more tension near the motor pulley, less at the far anchor point.
    This shows up as ringing artifacts that input shaping can reduce but not fully eliminate,
    because the frequency response varies with toolhead position. AWD distributes the drive force
    to both anchor points simultaneously, keeping belt tension consistent across the full travel.
    Community builders report measurably lower ringing residuals and the ability to push
    acceleration targets higher before print quality degrades.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tradeoff is real. An AWD gantry requires two additional stepper drivers — standard Voron
    2.4 electronics (Octopus, Spider) have enough spare drivers for this, but the wiring adds
    complexity. Klipper needs a custom <code>printer.cfg</code> section to define the paired motor
    relationships. You're also printing roughly 40 parts versus the standard gantry's 15–20, and
    every motor mount, idler bracket, and belt path must be squared to tighter tolerances than a
    two-motor system, because any asymmetry between paired motors on the same axis causes
    torque imbalance rather than simple skew.
  </p>
  <p>
    This mod is for builders who already run a well-tuned standard Voron 2.4 300mm and want to
    push further. If you haven't fully calibrated input shaping, pressure advance, and resonance
    compensation on your current gantry, that work should come first — the gains from AWD only
    become meaningful once the rest of the machine is dialled in. For builders at that level,
    the Mammoth-3D AWD Gantry 300 is one of the most thoroughly documented community AWD
    implementations available.
  </p>
</MotionSystemLayout>
